VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-015
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of right upper quadrant abdominal pain, severe lethargy, and a new-onset irregular heartbeat.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a combination of worsening upper abdominal pain, profound fatigue, and the sudden onset of palpitations in a patient with a complex chronic presentation. Immediate out-of-hours assessment was required to rule out acute hepatic decompensation, biliary tract emergencies, or a hemodynamically unstable arrhythmia before standard clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My upper stomach feels heavy and painful, my heart is suddenly fluttering constantly, and I simply don't have the energy to get out of bed."
History of Present Illness: Over the past week, the patient reports progressive, profound generalized fatigue and lethargy, culminating in being bedbound today. He describes an escalating, dull, continuous ache in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen. Over the last 4 hours, he developed a new-onset sensation of a rapid, irregular heartbeat (palpitations) accompanied by mild exertional dyspnoea. He reports a long-standing history of arthralgia predominantly affecting the second and third metacarpophalangeal joints. The adult child notes the patient has developed a diffuse "bronze" or slate-grey hyperpigmentation of the skin in sun-exposed areas over the last two years. He also reports recent new-onset diabetes mellitus and a loss of libido indicating hypogonadism.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to profound lethargy and new-onset cardiac symptoms.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of cardiac decompensation or acute liver failure.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 115 bpm (irregularly irregular) | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 96 percent on room air | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Chronically ill-appearing, fatigued, but alert. Striking slate-grey hyperpigmentation of the face and forearms.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a rapid, irregularly irregular heart rate with a pulse deficit, strongly suggestive of new-onset atrial fibrillation. No murmurs or gallops. Jugular venous pressure is mildly elevated. Abdominal exam reveals firm, non-tender hepatomegaly extending 4 cm below the right costal margin. Musculoskeletal exam notes bony enlargement and tenderness of the 2nd and 3rd MCP joints bilaterally. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: Profound fatigue/arthralgia, Bronze skin hyperpigmentation, New-onset diabetes/cirrhosis (hepatomegaly present), and anticipated Elevated transferrin saturation/ferritin pending urgent laboratory confirmation.
Environmental Observations: Patient is resting comfortably, but the layout requires climbing stairs, which is currently unsafe given the new arrhythmia.
Medication Reconciliation: Metformin for recently diagnosed diabetes. No rate-controlling or anticoagulant medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Haemochromatosis with new-onset atrial fibrillation and suspected hepatomegaly - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute viral hepatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of acute jaundice (bronzing is distinct), fever, and the chronicity of the fatigue and joint pains.

Biliary colic - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The abdominal pain is dull and constant rather than colicky, and the presence of systemic signs like profound hyperpigmentation and new arrhythmias point to a systemic infiltrative disease.

Primary osteoarthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The specific, atypical involvement of the 2nd and 3rd MCP joints, which is a hallmark of iron deposition disease rather than standard wear-and-tear osteoarthritis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute physical decline characterized by new-onset atrial fibrillation, firm hepatomegaly, and profound asthenia in the context of classic signs of severe iron overload (bronze diabetes). Triage accurately flagged the new-onset palpitations and worsening abdominal pain as high-risk. Objective assessment confirmed an uncontrolled irregular arrhythmia and clinical signs of early heart failure and liver infiltration. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed a severe risk of hemodynamic collapse or thromboembolic stroke. This mandated immediate out-of-hours evaluation to confirm the arrhythmia, assess cardiovascular stability, and arrange urgent medical admission, fulfilling the urgent MBS criteria.

