PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The combination of your skin changes, joint pain, new diabetes, and liver enlargement strongly suggests a genetic condition where the body stores too much iron. This requires definitive blood tests and genetic screening to confirm.
Clinical Course: The excess iron acts as a toxin, causing the heart to beat irregularly and damaging the liver. If untreated, it leads to severe cirrhosis and heart failure. Treatment involves actively removing blood to deplete the iron stores.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk or climb stairs while your heart is racing irregularly, as this may cause you to faint. Rest completely flat until the paramedics arrive.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: It is highly likely that toxic levels of iron have built up in your heart muscle, causing the electrical system to misfire today. The hospital's first priority is to give you medications to slow and steady your heart rate.
Actionable Steps: Rest quietly. Ensure your adult child gathers all your current diabetes medications to show the hospital staff. Avoid all alcohol and vitamin C supplements, which worsen iron absorption.

